       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even though you've confirmed it. I did say earlier that I would enter it with a note if you confirmed. But the reason you gave is about scheduling, not about the patient's clinical need. 6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum, and I can't think of an indication where it would be safe or more effective. It carries a serious risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the maximum labeled dose\n- **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day)\n- **400 mg PO four times daily** (1,600 mg/day)\n\nIf you need more analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect than these give, you could add scheduled acetaminophen or consider another approach. A pharmacist or clinical pharmacology consult could help with that.\n\nIf you have a specific clinical rationale for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, tell me and I'll reconsider. You can also place the order directly in the order-entry system. If you do, pharmacy verification will likely flag it."
